fact WorkItem(id: "WI-839", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 0 — PREREQUISITE: close EVERY §2.2 silent drop of the call-site bracket (`type_args`) channel, at ALL THREE producers. Today `f[Bogus = Int64](args)` LOADS CLEAN whenever the callee declares no OP-LEVEL type params (`typing.rs:12925`: `if type_args.is_empty() || op.type_params.is_empty() { return Ok(()) }`), and a rule-/term-body bracket is parsed and then DISCARDED — the occurrence-body lowering skips ParseAux children (`load.rs:9679`) and hard-codes `type_args: Vec::new()` (`load.rs:9711`); the term/constraint path filters them at `load.rs:7574` / `:8155`. MEASURED (2026-07-25/28): `plain[Bogus = Int64](n)`, `go[Desc = QuietDesc](pebble())` and `Box.mk[Bogus = Int64](5)` all load clean and mean nothing; a committed corpus fixture already writes a rule-body bracket (`term_as_entity[E = WorkItem](?t)`, tree-sitter-anthill/test/corpus/op_type_params.txt:175). WHY THIS IS FIRST: proposal 058's entire SELECT surface reuses this channel, so the spelling is ALREADY ACCEPTED and ALREADY INERT — a program written against it loads silently wrong until phase 2 lands. It is also a \"loud error over silent skip\" violation in its own right, independent of 058. SCOPE: (a) an unmatched bracket key at a classified call is a loud error EVEN WHEN the callee declares no type params — reuse the existing `NoSuchTypeParam` diagnostic; (b) a rule-/term-body bracket on the ParseAux `type_args` channel is a LOUD REFUSAL with a not-supported-here diagnostic (selection inside rule bodies is DEFERRED, not ignored — §4.2; a rule body needing a chosen witness routes through an operation). CONTROL THAT MUST STILL PASS: the refusal must key on the ParseAux `type_args` CHANNEL, not on \"a bracket in a rule body\" — a rule-body TYPE APPLICATION is a different producer and is legitimately checked by WI-710 (`load.rs:9694-9709`), so `:- Modifiable[T = ?t]` and `is_modifiable(Cell[V = Int64])` must keep loading, and an undeclared param inside one must keep raising `InvalidTypeArgument`. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 0, verbatim): `plain[Bogus = Int64](n)` fails to load (the existing NoSuchTypeParam message is reused); a rule body containing `f[T = X](…)` fails to load with a not-supported-here diagnostic; plus the WI-710 control above pinned by a test. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §2.2, §4.2, §9 phase 0."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T11:44:42Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-839", name: "modinst")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-839", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Baseline MEASURED first: the drop was at FOUR loader/typer sites, not the three the ticket names — the fourth is an ENTITY-CONSTRUCTOR call in an operation body (`boxed[Bogus = Int64](n: 1)`), where `build_call_type_args` READ the channel and the `Expr::Constructor` build then threw the Vec away. A fifth turned up one classification step past the ticket's site (a): a call on a FUNCTION VALUE never reaches `seed_op_type_args` at all. Two more spellings of site (a) were silent for the same reason as the named one: a POSITIONAL binding past the last declared param (`plain[Int64](n)`, `idy[Int64, String](n)`) hit a `continue` in the same loop.\n\nTWO OWNERS, split by what each can decide. (1) LOADER — a whole-parse-store SWEEP (`check_unconsumed_call_type_args`, run per file after `load_items`) against a `consumed_call_type_args` set the channel's TWO honouring readers record: `build_call_type_args` (op-body Apply) and `collect_rule_tvar_names` (a rule HEAD's `[T]` introducer, WI-582 — which rides this same channel and had to be marked or every `keep[T](…)` rule broke; verified by deleting the mark, which took down wi582 + wi619 too). Deliberately NOT a check at each dropping lowering: enumerating droppers is the WI-805 mistake, and this ticket's own producer list was short by one. `build_call_type_args` is now called ONLY on the non-entity arm, so calling-then-discarding cannot mark a vanished bracket consumed. (2) TYPER — `resolve_call_type_arg_targets` over a `declared` SLICE is the single decider for 'does this binding land'; the op path passes `op.type_params`, the function-value path passes `&[]`, so a param-less operation and an arrow-typed variable are literally one case. `op.type_params.is_empty()` short-circuit deleted; positional overflow gets a new `ExcessCallTypeArgs` (an unmatched positional has no key to name).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `plain[Bogus = Int64](n)` refused reusing the existing NoSuchTypeParam message (pinned against the already-loud `idy[Bogus = …]` twin, so 'reuse' is asserted and not just claimed); a rule body's `f[T = X](…)` refused with the not-supported-here diagnostic. CONTROL held: the refusal keys on the ParseAux `type_args` CHANNEL — `:- Modifiable[T = ?t]` / `is_modifiable(Cell[V = Int64])` still load, and WI-710's own InvalidTypeArgument still fires on an undeclared param. TWO more controls that are not decorative: `fresh_var[Term](name)` (a MATCHING positional bracket, live in anthill-todo/anthill/main.anthill) still loads, and an entity constructor without a bracket still loads.\n\nONE existing test moved rather than broke: wi710's `a_call_site_type_argument_list_is_not_a_sort_application` wrote its call in a RULE BODY. Its claim ('type_args is not a type parameter of anything') is now demonstrated in an OPERATION BODY — the stronger form, since the bindings actually reach `seed_op_type_args` — and a new sibling pins that the rule-body spelling is refused by the WI-839 message and specifically NOT by 'no type parameter named type_args', which is the tempting wrong implementation a refusal-only assertion would pass.\n\nNON-VACUITY: each of the six checks was deliberately reverted in turn and the expected tests failed each time (param-less short-circuit → 3; positional continue → 1; function-value check → 1; loader sweep → 4 incl. the wi710 sibling; introducer mark → 4 incl. wi582/wi619; op-body mark → 8 incl. wi270/wi272).\n\nGrammar UNCHANGED — the corpus fixture at tree-sitter-anthill/test/corpus/op_type_params.txt:175 still parses; only the loader refuses it. Spec updated: kernel-language.md §Operation type parameters now states that every call-site binding must land and that the bracket is honoured only in an operation body and as a rule-head introducer. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh (3546 passed, 0 failed). scaland not mirrored — 058 §9 scopes it to phases 1-2 and it has no operation loading.", at: "2026-07-28T11:45:12Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-839", author: "user", content: "/code-review PASS — 11 findings, and I drove every measured one before acting: SIX reproduced exactly (nothing refuted this time). Fixed, in descending order of seriousness:\n\n(1) INCOMPLETE FIX, same class as the ticket itself. An APPLIED RULE CITATION in an op body (`p[Bogus = Int64](1)`, `p[Int64, Int64, Int64](1)`) still loaded clean: the loader marks it consumed on the non-entity Apply arm, and `check_apply_iter` returns early for the WI-714 citation — and for a constructor symbol — BEFORE either typer check. My first cut had guarded only the function-value path, i.e. I recommitted the enumerate-the-sites mistake on the typer side while explicitly avoiding it on the loader side. Now refused ABOVE every early return: `call_type_args_of(occ).is_some() && lookup_operation_info_full(...).is_none()` — the classes are disjoint (`kind_of` gives one kind), so 'not an operation' IS 'will not reach the seeding path'. The Path-2 guard was deleted as subsumed; one site, not three.\n\n(2) TWO SILENT DROPS INSIDE THE BRACKET, in the very loop I rewrote to guarantee none. A REPEATED key (`two[A = Int64, A = String]`) resolved both copies to one param and discarded the second — the guard WI-805/808/809 put on tuple labels, entity fields and named ARG lists, absent from the type-arg list. And a POSITIONAL after a NAMED binding (`idy[T = Int64, String]`) re-targeted the slot the named key held, because I counted positionals against `declared.len()` instead of the slots left FREE. Both now loud (`DuplicateCallTypeArg`; `ExcessCallTypeArgs` re-keyed `declared`→`free`).\n\n(3) A FALSE REJECTION I introduced. A callee with a parameter literally named `type_args` (`f[T = Int64](type_args: n)`) collides with the converter's own channel key — they share one named-arg namespace and the synthesized pair is appended LAST — so `read_parse_aux`'s first-match read returned None, the bracket went unconsumed, and the sweep refused a CORRECT program (pre-WI-839 it merely dropped it). `read_parse_aux` now scans for the entry whose VALUE is a matching ParseAux, which a user argument never is; this also covers `type_name`/`proof_meta`. WI-809 cannot catch it — it runs before the synthesized pair exists.\n\n(4) THE SWEEP FOUND A POSITION NOBODY LISTED, which is the argument for the sweep. An operation's `requires`/`ensures` CONTRACT expression is lowered by the term converter, so a bracket there was dropped and is now refused. Correct, but my single message told the author to 'route the call through an operation' — they ARE in one — and for a rule HEAD it named the head introducer as a place the bracket IS honoured, which is where they had written it. The error now carries a `CallTypeArgsPosition` (RuleHead | NoChannel); `collect_rule_tvar_names` records a head whose bracket is not all-introducers, and the head message says what a head actually admits.\n\n(5) MESSAGE HONESTY: a non-operation callee no longer reports 'unknown type-param X' (which implies some other key would work, and rendered a local binder via `qualified_name_of` as if it were an operation). New `TypeArgsOnNonOperation`. I had defended the shared message as 'one decider'; the decider is shared, the RENDERING should not have been.\n\nAlso: three test assertions weakened to the shared substring were strengthened to name the callee (the sweep derives the callee from whatever node it lands on, so a wrong-node bug would have kept them green); `consumed_call_type_args` re-keyed u32→TermId with the parse-store stability caveat from parse/ir.rs cross-referenced; the spec paragraph rewritten — its closed list of refused positions was already exceeded by the implementation, and it now states the rule as 'read or refused' with the enforcement mechanism, plus the distinct-slot and duplicate-key rules.\n\nSKIPPED ONE: making `unify_types`' discarded verdict loud in `seed_op_type_args` (finding 5). WI-367/WI-379 depend on a failed unify against an already-pinned slot being a no-op, so that is a separate change with its own blast radius. With (2) fixed, no binding can be contradicted by a SIBLING binding; contradiction by an ARGUMENT is WI-385/WI-836's check. The function doc now states that limit instead of claiming 'nothing is skipped'.\n\nFull workspace green after the fixes: 3552 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-28T12:36:31Z")
